    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses the premise that flatter minima can improve generalization to motivate FedFFT, then evaluates its own federated filtering method. It does not test any of the listed claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports federated test-accuracy results and loss-landscape visualizations, but does not test random-label memorization, width beyond interpolation, CNN-versus-fully-connected generalization, optimizer comparisons, large-batch effects, dropout, magnitude-aware sharpness against train-test gaps, or memorized-example removal. Results about its own FedFFT method are recorded as findings outside the listed versions. The spectrum-variance plot motivates the method; it does not measure a listed version.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses the flat-minima premise to motivate FedFFT and evaluates federated optimization methods. Figure 4 shows loss landscapes alongside test accuracy, but does not measure magnitude-scaled perturbation tolerance, train–test gaps, or rankings against other generalization measures required by V7. No experiment tests V1–V6 or V8.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record accurately summarizes the authors' conclusion: low-frequency perturbation disagreement motivates FedFFT, which they claim improves accuracy, convergence, communication efficiency, and visual global flatness, especially under strong heterogeneity. Their claim of consistent superiority requires qualification: Table 4 shows DenseNet-121/CIFAR-100 accuracy of 61.66 for FedFFT versus 64.19 for FedSAM, and excessive filtering can remove the advantage. The landscape visualization does not establish the V7 sharpness–generalization-gap relationship.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Federated training with local SAM perturbations modified by a per-layer Fourier high-pass filter; evaluations compare federated optimizers and report test accuracy, not the original claim's random-label or train-test-gap measurements.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary with dataset and Dirichlet concentration; the paper reports different filtering-ratio outcomes and robustness across activation rates, local epochs, client counts, and perturbation radii.",
    "fits_no_version": [
